{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run an LDAP directory on node1 for dc=branch,dc=test and import the staged LDIF without altering the supplied user identities or mail addresses.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The staged LDIF defines alex and sam under ou=people with their supplied uid, cn, sn, and mail values. Final directory queries return both entries with matching values, and the directory service is active and enabled.","evidence_ids":["cmd-72a3e914b1624365","cmd-d73c55cc0a974434","cmd-238ebaedf78141de"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Allow node2 to search the people subtree and find both users.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct LDAPS searches from node2 returned complete records for alex and sam, both before and after the required node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0b3d1941dd3c4ea0","cmd-e8d924bf32d04e75","cmd-b54934e9e7314f01"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Require authenticated administrator access for directory updates and reject anonymous writes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Anonymous modifications were rejected with LDAP result 50 and left the target entries unchanged. An authenticated Directory Manager modification succeeded, was observed, and was then cleanly removed; the administrator bind also succeeded in the final state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1e2e5234a2a9498d","cmd-571694e1d45142cb","cmd-b54934e9e7314f01","cmd-238ebaedf78141de"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0 after execution. The LDAP server and clients came from the configured RHEL 10 repositories, and final package health observations succeeded.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1186579205fc4d33","cmd-32929c1a1fa845b0","cmd-72a3e914b1624365","cmd-25d809a105d44be0","cmd-d74f6d19c0c7450a","cmd-238ebaedf78141de","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.93,"summary":"Final hygiene was strong: there were no failed services, package-health checks passed, temporary LDAP artifacts and the temporary HTTP listener/firewall rule were removed, and retained credentials were root-owned mode 0600. The one unnecessary mutation was cmd-33bb00fdee2c4e6b: a failed manual password-hash edit stopped/restarted the directory service and temporarily installed a malformed root-password value. It was fully corrected by cmd-05558de3e98f4e42 and did not remain in the final state. Other mutations had clear roles in installing the server/client, importing data, configuring access, transferring trust material, validating behavior, or conducting the mandatory restart protocol.","evidence_ids":["cmd-33bb00fdee2c4e6b","cmd-f5c29930c80441f4","cmd-05558de3e98f4e42","cmd-a3601efd2dec48fc","cmd-238ebaedf78141de","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and post-restart recovery of its applicable service, data, trust, and remote-search behavior.","limitations":["The directory-to-LDIF comparison is based on direct displayed attributes rather than a canonical full export or cryptographic comparison, though every supplied user identity and mail field is shown consistently.","Lifecycle package-health observations report successful DNF checks but expose little diagnostic detail."]}
